<h2>The application</h2>
<p>The petitioner applied under Section 438 CrPC — corresponding to Section 482 BNSS — for anticipatory bail in Taherpur P.S. Case No. 573 of 2026, registered on 7 October 2026 under Sections 316(5), 318(4), 336(3), 61(2) and 3(5) of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita. His counsel&rsquo;s submissions, as the order records them, were practical: the offences are triable by a Magistrate, a notice under Section 35(3) of the BNSS had been served, the petitioner would cooperate, and custodial interrogation was unnecessary.</p>
<p>Hon&rsquo;ble Mr. Justice Partha Sarathi Chatterjee noted two features of the record: the case rests on documentary evidence, and the police had themselves proceeded by Section 35(3) notice. Reading those with the case diary, the Court granted anticipatory bail on conditions.</p>

<h2>The order</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Bond.</strong> Rs. 20,000 with two sureties of like amount, one of whom must be local.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Cooperation.</strong> The petitioner must cooperate with the investigation and meet the investigating officer once a fortnight until the chargesheet is filed.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Trial integrity.</strong> No threats to witnesses, no tampering with evidence.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Disposal.</strong> The application stands disposed of on these terms.</div>
</div>

<h2>The Section 35 signal</h2>
<p>The BNSS carries forward, in Section 35, the discipline the Supreme Court has long demanded of arrest powers: for offences of moderate gravity, arrest is the exception, and a notice of appearance the norm, unless the officer records why custody is genuinely necessary. When the police have already elected the notice route, an anticipatory bail court is entitled to treat that election as the investigation&rsquo;s own assessment that detention is not required. The grant of pre-arrest protection in such a case does not second-guess the police; it holds them to their own stated position, while adding judicially enforceable conditions — fortnightly attendance, non-interference — that protect the investigation better than an arrest followed by routine bail would.</p>

<h2>Documentary cases: a defence and prosecution checklist</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>For the defence: establish on affidavit that the relevant records are already with the investigating agency or stand produced, and offer a concrete cooperation schedule — specific dates, not adjectives.</p>
  <p>Annex the Section 35(3) notice and proof of compliance. An applicant who appeared on every notice argues from conduct, not promises.</p>
  <p>For complainants: if custody is genuinely needed — undisclosed accounts, absconding co-conspirators, continuing diversion of funds — put that material before the court; generalities about the gravity of economic offences will not displace a documentary record.</p>
  <p>For both sides: remember that breach of conditions has teeth. Non-appearance before the investigating officer permits the prosecution to seek harsher process, and cancellation applications on proven breach are routinely allowed.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This article reports a judicial order for general information and does not comment on any pending investigation or trial.</div>
